#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Bitcoin block explorer for Umbrel, on your own Bitcoin node (Python standard library only).

One file for both Bitcoin chains: CHAIN=sha256 (the Bitcoin Node app, "Bitcoin Explorer" at btc.hashes.space) or
CHAIN=blake2b (the Bitcoin Knots app on the BLAKE2b chain, "Bitcoin BLAKE2b Explorer" at btcb2.hashes.space).
Reads everything from the node's RPC, pruned or not, and serves:

  /                      the node's state, the latest blocks and the mempool
  /block/<hash|height>   a block with its transactions (a block the pruned node no longer keeps is not listed)
  /tx/<txid>             a transaction (mempool, a block you came from, or any tx with -txindex)
  /search?q=             height, block hash or txid
  /api/v1/blocks         the last blocks, in mempool's format, with the fees and the pool worked out from your node
  /api/v1/fees/mempool-blocks   the blocks your mempool would build, best fee rate first
  /api/blocks/tip/height
  /api/v1/mining/hashrate/<period>  global hashrate and difficulty, from the block headers (24h, 14d, 1m, 6m, 1y; epoch: since the last difficulty adjustment)
  /api/v1/mining/pools/<period>     blocks per mining pool, from the tags and payout addresses in each coinbase (period epoch: the blocks since the last difficulty adjustment)
  /api/v1/mining/pool-hashrate/<period>  the hashrate of the mining pools together: the network's hashrate times the share of blocks that pools mined, per day
  /api/v1/mining/solo/<period>      the hashrate of the miners that use no pool: blocks of solo services ("solo" in the pool's name) and blocks with no pool tag or known payout address, times the network's hashrate
  /api/v1/mining/pool/<slug>/blocks   the latest blocks of one pool (the page's pool view); .../blocks/epoch: every block of the pool since the last difficulty adjustment, with fees
  /api/v1/mining/block/<hash|height>  the pool information of one block: pool, coinbase text and who the reward was paid to (the pool page on hashes.space opens it)
  /api/v1/difficulty-adjustment       where the chain is in its difficulty period, and the change the period is heading for
  /api/v1/mining/period-blocks/<height>  up to 15 blocks from that height down to the first block of the difficulty period (the blockchain on hashes.space swipes back through them)
  /api/v1/mining/next-block           when the next block is expected: the average wait, worked out from the pace of the last 288 blocks and the next block's difficulty
  /api/v1/fees/recommended            fastest / half-hour / hour / economy / minimum fee rates, from your own mempool
  /api/v1/halving                     blocks and time to the next halving
  /api/v1/network                     the node: version, peers, mempool size and minimum fee, disk use, pruning
  /api/v1/block/<hash|height>/stats   per-block statistics: fees, fee rates, SegWit and Taproot share, OP_RETURN, total sent
  /api/v1/blocks/history              block size, fees and fee rates of the blocks the node keeps (the charts on the home page)
  /api/v1/chain-tips                  chain forks and stale blocks (getchaintips); /forks is the page
  /api/v1/tx/<txid>/projection        where a mempool transaction lands (block, ETA), its CPFP package and whether it can be replaced
  /api/v1/address/<address>           balance and unspent outputs of one address (scantxoutset: slow, runs in the background, cached)
  /fee-auction           the waiting transactions as bids: ranked by fee rate, where they land and the price of the next block (/fee-auction.json: the same as data)
  /package-sorting       how your node fills the block: packages of transactions ranked by fee rate (/package-sorting.json: the same as data)
  /block-assembly        the block your node would build right now: the coinbase, the picked transactions, the limits and the 80-byte header (/block-assembly.json: the same as data)
  /api/v1/block-template              the header of the block being mined now (getblocktemplate): previous block, merkle root of the template, bits, time; the nonce stays null
  /api/v1/ws             mempool's WebSocket: {"action":"want","data":["blocks","mempool-blocks"]}
  /api/v1/status         what the explorer sees of the node (for when something does not show up)

Fees are in sat/vB. The explorer refuses to serve (the page then shows simulated blocks) while the node is still syncing, because a node that is behind would show old blocks as live.
"""

